"A container diagram decomposes one system into applications and data stores with explicit responsibilities and communication paths.\n\nUse when: Explain major executable and storage boundaries to developers. Review how responsibilities and technologies are distributed.\n\nAvoid when: The main question concerns host placement or replica counts.\n\nVisual grammar: Containers encode independently running applications or data stores, not necessarily Docker containers. A padded system boundary encloses owned containers. Directed relationships name the action and relevant protocol.\n\nBuild: Start from an agreed context diagram and retain its external actors. Identify applications and stores without splitting them into classes. Give each container a domain name, responsibility, and technology subtitle. Arrange the principal path left to right, routing secondary links through open corridors.\n\nCheck: Each application has a clear purpose and storage ownership is apparent. Protocols are supported by configuration or labeled as proposed. Environment-specific replication details are absent. Boundary titles, container labels, and arrows have nonoverlapping space.\n\nPitfalls: Confusing a C4 container with only an operating-system container. Using an unlabeled database shared by unrelated responsibilities.\n\nExample: A booking system contains Web App, Booking API, Worker, and Booking Store, linked by HTTPS and job messages.\n\nAlternatives: Component diagram, Deployment diagram."
